WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:06.000
Are the Borg really beyond saving? When&nbsp;
can you trust an omnipotent trickster?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:10.800
Captain Jean-Luc Picard had some pretty heavy&nbsp;
decisions to make aboard the USS Enterprise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:13.440
keep watching to see the absolute best!
 
 

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:18.880
In the series premiere of "Star Trek: The&nbsp;
Next Generation," Riker first meets his new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:24.240
captain in a ready room and is given his first&nbsp;
order right away. It's an order given sternly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:29.280
and sets the tone for Picard as a cold,&nbsp;
by-the-book commanding officer. But over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:34.400
course of the series, the two would form one of&nbsp;
just a few genuine friendships for Captain Picard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:38.960
and his choice to appoint him his first&nbsp;
officer would alter the trajectory of his life.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:43.520
In that same first episode, one of Picard's&nbsp;
next acts as captain was to instruct his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:48.240
new number one to prevent him from "making an&nbsp;
ass" of himself with the children on the ship.

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:53.360
"I'm not a family man, Riker. And yet Starfleet&nbsp;
has given me a ship with children aboard."

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:56.880
Over the course of the series,&nbsp;
Riker would help Picard go from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:00.560
a stoic stick-in-the-mud who preferred&nbsp;
to stay an arm's length from his crew,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.760
to a man with friendly relationships&nbsp;
with those under his command.
 
 
 &nbsp;

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:08.080
One could argue that the mere act of&nbsp;
recruiting Data could be one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:13.120
boldest decisions Picard ever made, despite&nbsp;
the android having a long and distinguished&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:17.440
service record that would warrant just about&nbsp;
any starship captain bringing him aboard.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:22.080
But while other officers may have seen Data as&nbsp;
a mere machine, Captain Picard and those on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:27.280
Enterprise learned to see him as so much more,&nbsp;
even though visitors were routinely surprised.

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:32.960
"I must admit, Commander, I didn't&nbsp;
think you'd be so… personable."

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:37.440
Aboard the Enterprise, Picard and his crew&nbsp;
saw Data as a person, and when his rights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:42.160
as an individual came under attack, they leapt&nbsp;
to his defense. And when Data was ordered to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:47.120
be turned over to Starfleet as property in "The&nbsp;
Measure Of A Man," it was Picard who was at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:52.240
forefront of the fight, demanding they could&nbsp;
judge and decide his status. It was Picard who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:57.280
acted as his defense counsel, in one of the most&nbsp;
important acts he'd perform as captain, an act&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:01.920
with consequences that continue to reverberate&nbsp;
through the "Star Trek'' universe even today.
 
 

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:04.480
In the first season of "The Next Generation",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:09.680
there didn't seem to be any set chief engineer on&nbsp;
the Enterprise. But in the second season premiere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:13.680
Picard would promote helmsman Geordi La&nbsp;
Forge to the permanent spot at the head of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:18.320
the ship's engine room. To audiences it may have&nbsp;
seemed like an odd jump for the young officer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:22.800
but it proved to be one of the key changes&nbsp;
on the ship after its first rocky year.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:26.080
La Forge went on to become one&nbsp;
of the standouts on the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:30.320
and was even ranked by ScreenRant as&nbsp;
the franchise's best engineering chiefs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:35.520
He'd solve crisis after crisis — including&nbsp;
several galactic threats — and it never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:39.520
would have been possible if he'd stayed a mere&nbsp;
pilot, or been moved to a different post.
 
 

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:45.760
One of the most unexpected turns of events in the&nbsp;
entire run of "Star Trek: The Next Generation"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:51.120
occurred in the third season episode "Sins Of The&nbsp;
Father." Essentially a sci-fi political drama,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:55.120
the episode brought the action to the Klingon&nbsp;
homeworld with the sudden accusation of treason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.120 --> 00:03:00.240
made against Worf's dead father. Turning up on&nbsp;
the Enterprise is Worf's long-lost brother Kurn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:03.760
who wants Worf to travel home and defend&nbsp;
their family's honor. But when Kurn is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:08.000
the victim of an assassination attempt,&nbsp;
Worf finds himself alone — and Captain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:12.560
Picard steps in to help. Not as captain of&nbsp;
the Enterprise, but as a trusted friend.

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:16.960
With Picard's assistance, new evidence is&nbsp;
discovered, along with a direct eyewitness that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:22.160
clears Worf's father of the crime. To prevent a&nbsp;
civil war however, Worf takes the fall, but averts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:26.560
a death sentence — thanks to the evidence Picard&nbsp;
revealed — leaving justice for another day.
 
 

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:31.120
In season three's "Hollow Pursuits" we&nbsp;
met a very unusual Starfleet Officer:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:36.000
Lieutenant Reginald Barclay. Barclay was far&nbsp;
from the steady, skilled, and capable men&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:40.960
and women of Starfleet. Barclay was awkward,&nbsp;
clumsy, and lacking in confidence; he was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:46.320
social outcast who struggled to fit in among the&nbsp;
more gregarious and bold crew of the Enterprise.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:50.560
"I've examined Barclay's psychological profile.&nbsp;
He has a history of seclusive tendencies."

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:56.000
When it became known that he wasn't measuring&nbsp;
up, Commander Riker wanted him off the ship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:59.440
insisting he had no place on the&nbsp;
Enterprise, and suspecting that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:03.680
Barclay's previous commanders might have given&nbsp;
him recommendations just to get rid of him.

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:08.640
But just when it seemed like Picard might follow&nbsp;
Barclay's previous captains and send him packing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:13.120
he decided to do something nobody had done&nbsp;
before: reach out to Barclay with a caring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:17.600
compassionate helping hand. Instead of&nbsp;
transferring the so-called problem officer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:20.800
Picard instructed Geordi to&nbsp;
"become his best friend."

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:23.760
"With all due respect sir, my best friend?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:27.440
I can barely tolerate being in&nbsp;
the same room with the man."

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:31.680
Eventually, the crew came to appreciate&nbsp;
Barclay for his unique talents and skills,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:34.400
and together helped him come&nbsp;
out of his shell. Eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:38.880
he'd help bring the stranded crew of Voyager&nbsp;
home safely, saving hundreds of lives.
 
 

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:44.560
The mischievous Q was initially presented as&nbsp;
a malevolent, godlike entity with sinister&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:50.160
but mysterious motives. He tormented the crew&nbsp;
in the premiere episode and popped up again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:55.840
throughout the series to cause mischief&nbsp;
and mayhem aboard the USS Enterprise.

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:59.040
"Au contraire, mon capitaine! He's back!"

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:02.480
But it's in the third-season episode "Deja Q"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:06.800
that Picard makes his most important&nbsp;
decision regarding the all-powerful Q.

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:10.560
Exiled from his own people, Q&nbsp;
was turned into a mere mortal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:15.280
and sought refuge on the Enterprise. When he&nbsp;
became the target of a vengeful alien race,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:19.360
Picard chose to help him, and&nbsp;
provided Q with a safe haven. Mostly.

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:24.320
"Seems human enough to me."

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:29.840
It wasn't an easy call, and few would have blamed&nbsp;
him if he hadn't agreed to come to his aid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:35.200
particularly as Q's presence proved to be&nbsp;
a danger to the ship. But helping Q showed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:40.800
the trickster how special humans — and Picard —&nbsp;
really were. Q later felt he owed Jean-Luc a debt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:46.880
of gratitude he'd repay in "Qpid," "Tapestry,"&nbsp;
and in the series finale, "All Good Things,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:53.120
helping him learn life lessons and save mankind,&nbsp;
too. Had Picard not intervened, we also may never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:58.960
have witnessed the Q civil war on "Voyager" — nor&nbsp;
his return in Season 2 of "Star Trek: Picard."
 
 

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:04.160
"Who Watches The Watchers" may be one of the&nbsp;
most quintessential episodes of "Star Trek:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:08.560
The Next Generation." It all starts when a&nbsp;
secret Starfleet research team is discovered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:13.600
by a pre-industrial society called the Mintakans,&nbsp;
and in the ensuing confusion where the Enterprise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:18.640
comes to assist, begin to believe that Picard&nbsp;
is an all-powerful god. The prime directive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:23.680
demands they not interfere, but the damage is&nbsp;
done, and while the Starfleet expert advises&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:28.480
they hand down a set of religious tenets to&nbsp;
mitigate the damage, Picard outright refuses.

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:32.640
Instead, the captain speaks directly to the&nbsp;
primitive people and plainly explains the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:37.200
situation. In the end, the Mintakans begin&nbsp;
to understand the truth of what happened,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:40.400
and agree to go their separate&nbsp;
ways — but not before gifting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:43.600
Picard with a hand-woven tapestry&nbsp;
to remember their time together.
 
 

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:46.880
One of the major guest stars&nbsp;
to appear on "Star Trek:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:51.600
The Next Generation" who originated in "The&nbsp;
Original Series" was Mr. Spock's father Sarek.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:56.560
The elder Vulcan Ambassador had appeared in the&nbsp;
original series, and in three of the five feature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.560 --> 00:07:01.440
films before he showed up on TNG in the season&nbsp;
three episode, "Sarek." Coming aboard the ship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:06.160
to conduct final negotiations with a mysterious&nbsp;
alien race, it's soon discovered that the aging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:10.640
Vulcan is afflicted with a rare disease that&nbsp;
is sapping him of his mental abilities — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.640 --> 00:07:15.920
his logic. In the climax of the episode, Picard&nbsp;
selflessly agrees to a dangerous mind meld with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:20.560
Sarek in order to help stabilize his condition&nbsp;
so he can follow through with his final mission.

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:23.680
The meld is a success, but&nbsp;
not without consequences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:29.840
and Picard undergoes some&nbsp;
serious trauma as a result.

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:30.480
"Give me back my control."

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:34.160
Picard's joining with Sarek's&nbsp;
mind gives him a more centered and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:39.280
measured sense of control. It also gave him&nbsp;
a profound connection to Ambassador Spock,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:44.720
who he'd meet — and meld with — in the episode&nbsp;
"Unification," which in turn may even have helped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:48.800
fuel his desire to help the Romulans in&nbsp;
the follow-up "Star Trek: Picard." 
 
 

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:52.400
When the Enterprise first made contact&nbsp;
with the Borg they were shown to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:56.880
little more than a robotic hive mind, intent&nbsp;
on absorbing life into their collective.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:01.920
But in the episode "I, Borg," Doctor Crusher&nbsp;
convinces Picard to bring aboard an injured and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:06.560
dying Borg they find at the site of a crashed&nbsp;
Borg ship. Initially, Picard wants to use the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:11.840
drone as a weapon against the collective, using a&nbsp;
computer virus to sabotage their entire network.

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:13.360
"A terminal one."

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:20.000
But as the Borg recovers, it begins to show signs&nbsp;
of individuality, eventually taking the name Hugh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:23.120
and showing an unusually strong moral compass.

00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:28.480
Ultimately, Picard chooses to respect Hugh's right&nbsp;
to autonomy and lets Hugh make his own choice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:33.440
about his future. Hugh decides to return to the&nbsp;
collective, and Picard's greatest hope is realized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:40.240
: that Hugh's sense of self and morality could&nbsp;
be just as contagious to the Borg as any virus.
 
 

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:44.720
Throughout the fourth season, trouble was brewing&nbsp;
in the Klingon Empire, beginning with the death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:49.520
of the former Chancellor K'mpec. It's determined&nbsp;
that Duras — the same man who had framed Worf's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:53.840
father — is guilty of the assassination, as&nbsp;
well as the murder of Worf's ex-girlfriend.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:57.840
That's enough reason for Worf&nbsp;
to kill Duras in ritual combat.

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:03.680
The mysterious Gowron is named the new chancellor.&nbsp;
But Duras' powerful family haunts the Empire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:09.040
and it all comes to a head in the two-part season&nbsp;
ender, "Redemption." With Gowron about to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.040 --> 00:09:13.360
his place as leader of his people, the House of&nbsp;
Duras, with the outside help from the Romulans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:18.160
attempts to take control themselves.&nbsp;
Torn between his people and his duty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:23.440
Worf sides with Gowron and asks that&nbsp;
Picard — and Starfleet — join him.

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:27.840
Unable to get directly involved in what&nbsp;
is deemed "an internal Klingon affair,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:32.080
Picard instead hatches a plan to expose the&nbsp;
Romulan alliance with the House of Duras.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:36.800
The plan works, Gowron is installed as Chancellor&nbsp;
thanks to Picard's help, and Worf finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:42.240
regains the honor he had lost in "Sins Of The&nbsp;
Father." It's a bold and risky move for Picard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:47.120
but it sets off a chain of events that would have&nbsp;
after effects for years, spilling over into "Deep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.120 --> 00:09:52.880
Space Nine" years later, as Gowron owes much of&nbsp;
his legacy to Starfleet and Picard's assistance.
 
 

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:59.040
Recruited by a corrupt admiral to help locate&nbsp;
a supposed Bajoran terrorist, Ensign Ro Laren&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:04.480
was brought to the Enterprise against Picard's&nbsp;
explicit objections. But when Ensign Ro uncovered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:09.600
a plot to supply Cardassian militants with illegal&nbsp;
weapons, she didn't know who to trust. With&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:14.560
help from Guinan, Ensign Ro placed her faith in&nbsp;
Captain Picard to help her out of the situation.

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:18.160
"Guinan is very selective&nbsp;
about who she calls a friend."

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:22.320
Rather than toss her into the brig for&nbsp;
taking part in a corruption scandal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:26.400
Picard — impressed by her integrity —&nbsp;
asks her to remain on the Enterprise.

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:29.920
It may have seemed like an obvious&nbsp;
choice for viewers in the moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.920 --> 00:10:33.440
who saw Ensign Ro distinguish herself&nbsp;
in the episode, but she was coming from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:37.840
a Federation penal colony, and her&nbsp;
reputation was less than stellar.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.840 --> 00:10:42.640
Giving her a position on the bridge of the&nbsp;
flagship of the Federation was a risky call,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:49.680
and it paid off in spades, as Ro became a&nbsp;
standout officer and a fan-favorite character.
 
 

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:54.000
When "Star Trek: The Next Generation" first&nbsp;
debuted in 1987 it was living in the shadow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:58.480
of the original "Star Trek" series, and&nbsp;
Picard himself was being measured harshly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.480 --> 00:11:03.440
against his counterpart, James T. Kirk as played&nbsp;
by franchise legend William Shatner. Thankfully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:08.800
Patrick Stewart played the role masterfully, and&nbsp;
Picard eventually became equally as beloved in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:14.800
own way. In the first "Next Generation" film in&nbsp;
1994, "Star Trek: Generations," the two captains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:19.280
of the Enterprise would come face to face,&nbsp;
thanks to a fateful decision by Captain Picard.

00:11:19.280 --> 00:11:23.920
Both captains become trapped in a mysterious,&nbsp;
time-bending anomaly called "The Nexus."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:28.560
When Picard attempts to escape and stop the&nbsp;
villainous Soran, he discovers Kirk inside the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:34.400
Nexus, and recruits him for the task. Together&nbsp;
they stop Soran, and in the melee, Kirk dies at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:40.000
Soran's hands, but saves millions of lives in the&nbsp;
process. The decision to bring Kirk back for one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:45.920
more mission may be the most important choice&nbsp;
he ever made. Not just to save lives, but to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:51.680
give Trekkies the moment they'd been waiting for:&nbsp;
the meeting of the two best Enterprise captains.

